From 72bc81b8865e90496fda3fb45811e63f3c793af6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:07:29 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] wip/host-expert-pool-region: the pool IS the
 expert-indexed region, and any device can read it in place

TODO #139.  The design of record asks for "one pinned host region, addressed by expert
index, with a presence bitmap, so the two staging branches collapse into one hole-aware
path".  That region already exists -- it is the bounded pinned host pool: a per-host-tensor
pinned allocation addressed by (expert -> slot), with `slot_state` as the presence bitmap,
`base_dev` as the UVA alias, ranked by the heat prior, and a native fill source (a resident
fill is a 1.3 us D2D from the alias; a hole is a page-cache/disk fill).  What it lacked was
the right to be a COLD-READ source away from one GPU, so on a tensor split every resident
expert's access still paid an arena fill and an H2D.

Two restrictions blocked that, and both are geometry accidents rather than properties of
the pool:

  * `device_count == 1` -- but `base_dev` is a Portable|Mapped UVA alias that is already
    used as the cross-device D2D source for fills;
  * `src_off == 0` -- but a tensor split's slice offset is folded into the cold base for a
    pinned master already (`moe_cache_get_cold`), so do the same for the pool.

Both now live in ONE predicate, `pool_cold_geometry_ok()`, used by the host-side admission
decision (`pool_cold_slot_locked`) AND the kernel-side cold description
(`moe_cache_get_cold`): if the two disagreed the host would emit a cold id the kernel
resolves from a different base, i.e. a silently wrong matmul.  Fused tables stay excluded
(one stride cannot express several ranges).  Kill switch `MOE_HOST_POOL_COLD_MULTIDEV=0`
restores the old restriction exactly.

And `-lme N` now spends the budget where it is worth more: below 90% of the host expert set
it sizes the POOL (per-EXPERT residency, ranked by the heat prior) instead of pinning whole
tensors, because MoE routing is spread across every layer so no whole tensor is hot.  At or
above 90% it keeps the whole-tensor pinned-master tier: a fully resident pool still keeps a
pageable master, and only a pinned master gets the single-launch staging branch and the
native fill.  `MOE_HOST_TIER=pool|tensor` forces either shape (the A/B arm).  The explicit
budget reaches the pool through a new device-API call, `moe_cache_set_host_pool_budget()`.

Measured, 2 devices (0,2), 35B-A3B Q4_K_M -ncmoe 40, -n 300, prose 5246, text identical
(`sha=231512fc77e5`) in every pair:

  arm                              prefill  decode   cold reaches
  -lme 8   MULTIDEV=0 (old)          ~505*    48.9   0
  -lme 8   MULTIDEV=1 (new)          654.0    54.5   76878
  -lme 12  pool / whole tensors        --     61.6 / 56.8
  -lme 16  pool / whole tensors        --     65.6 / 65.7
  -lme 17  pool / whole tensors        --     67.0 / 68.2
  -lme 64  (>= the set -> tensors)   1106.0    71.6
  pool FRAC 50 (not-fitting band)    714.2    57.0   84714   (was 51.8 without)
  pool FRAC 100 (full coverage)     1166.4    63.9  105342

1 device (FRAC 100) 51.4, 3 devices -sm layer 47.4 (was refused there entirely), 3 devices
-sm tensor 19.5 (the TODO #135 degenerate split).  check-gpu-faults clean on every arm; the
3-device tensor split is byte-identical with the switch on and off (sha=d589ad909b67), which
is the sliced/2-D geometry check.
